./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1016:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, NO generic Update, and NO soft-delete column** — only Insert + Disable lifecycle flip (BE-0432 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0428 user_tenant_isolation_test.go and BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shapes are the templates, but four substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has its OWN id (no shared-identity column the way memberships shares user_id with the global users table) and the load-bearing cross-row failure mode is "two tenants happen to share a slug (UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) permits this by design) and a WHERE-on-slug-only or WHERE-on-id-only mutation swaps the WRONG tenant's row": (a) every byte-identical-snapshot test seeds BOTH tenants with service accounts holding the SAME slug via a `seedServiceAccountWithSlug(t, db, f, org, label, slug)` helper that overrides the factory's slug — a WHERE-on-slug mistake would resolve to whichever row the planner found first and the per-tenant id assertion would catch it (the BE-0430 "same user, distinct role/role_version tuples" framing translates here to "different ids, SAME slug"; the inverse is the only cross-row leak surface a tenant-root-with-its-own-id table exposes); (b) the Disable bystander test is the analogue of BE-0430's UpdateRole bystander — Disable is the only mutation beyond Insert, so the BE-0428 generic Update test does not apply; the trigger dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed) PLUS the lifecycle field (disabled_at == nil) on the bystander row catch any WHERE-on-slug-only Disable that ever matched the wrong row; (c) the duplicate-Insert Conflict bystander test snapshots BOTH the conflicting target tenant's row AND the same-slug-different-tenant bystander row AND a post-state CountByOrganization == 1 for each (since the trigger is BEFORE-UPDATE only, a rolled-back INSERT that resolved its UNIQUE check against the cross-tenant row would have no trigger anchor — Count is the second observable surface a row landing in the wrong tenant could leak through, mirroring the BE-0431 invariants-file Count-after-rollback rule); (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND Disable (the only two NotFound-emitting paths the repository exposes) — divergence in Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the mutation path is a separate code path from the read; afterward, re-read the foreign tenant's row and assert disabled_at == nil so the cross-tenant Disable probe did not silently flip the lifecycle. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run (mirrors BE-0430): (i) disjoint-fixture count isolation (orgA=1 SA, orgB=3 SAs, gotA must contain only saA's id, gotB must contain exactly {saB1, saB2, saB3} — proves no JOIN scrambled which rows land where); (ii) shared-slug-row isolation (saA and saB both with slug "shared-ci-slug", List(orgA) returns exactly one row with id=saA, List(orgB) returns exactly one row with id=saB — proves a SELECT disambiguating by slug instead of by organization would have either swapped or merged the two rows). One additional CountByOrganization-specific test seeds three tenants (orgA=2, orgB=3, orgC=0 empty bystander) and asserts the three distinct counts; this is the quota-layer leak surface (Count is the read the quota engine consults to enforce per-organization limits, so a leak inflates one tenant's effective quota with another's usage). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (service_accounts has no deletion_scheduled_at — only disabled_at, which the byte-identical Disable snapshot treats as the analogue) AND the deliberate ABSENCE of generic-Update / DELETE / cascade tests (Disable is the only mutation; the api_keys.service_account_id cross-tenant FK leak proof lives in serviceaccount_test.go's TestServiceAccountAPIKeyCannotCrossTenantBoundary; the cascade-on-org-delete proof lives in TestServiceAccountBelongsToOneOrganization and TestTenantHierarchyCascadeDelete) in the file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0428/BE-0430's deliberate-absence note). Note on apierr.NotFound message vs Hint: NotFound("service_account", id) Newf-builds a Message containing the caller-supplied id verbatim, but never sets Hint — so the cross-tenant-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability check MUST compare Code and Hint only (Message would diverge by caller input, which is not a leak). Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant-isolation: same shape with the same "two tenants share a slug-equivalent" angle — api_keys has UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix), so the shared-prefix fixture is the structural counterpart; plus a secret-bearing extension — the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface) and BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant-isolation).
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1017:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, THREE distinct UPDATE paths (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential), an additional UPDATE-but-not-mutation-state path (TouchLastUsed), and a secret-bearing row body (secret_hash + globally-UNIQUE prefix)** (BE-0433 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go, BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go, and BE-0431 serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but five substitutions are mandatory because api_keys has no version column, three UPDATE paths instead of one, a separate touch-style UPDATE for the auth path, and a secret-bearing row body: (a) the trigger-dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE UPDATE trigger) must run ONCE PER UPDATE PATH — UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, and TouchLastUsed each get their own dedicated dual-anchor test, so a refactor that dropped the trigger from one path but not the others trips a path-specific gate (the BE-0431 single-Disable pattern is too weak here); (b) the UpdateMutable dual-anchor test additionally pins the **mutable surface** to exactly {name, scopes} by snapshotting every non-mutable column (prefix, secret_hash, created_by, service_account_id, expires_at, revoked_at, last_used_at) as byte-identical to the baseline — UpdateMutable is the first repository method in the store package whose SET-clause is a true subset of the table's mutable columns, so the non-mutation assertion is the only structural defense against a future refactor that quietly widened the SET-clause; (c) RotateCredential gets a dedicated test that pins the **credential swap** invariant: prefix and secret_hash flip to the new values, every identity/ownership/scopes/lifecycle column stays byte-identical, the OLD pr