./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1018:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row whose mutation surface spans FIVE distinct methods (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed plus the Insert path) AND whose row body carries TWO load-bearing credential primitives (a globally-UNIQUE prefix and a secret-bearing secret_hash)** (BE-0434 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_tenant_isolation_test.go; the cross-tenant analogue of BE-0433 and the secret-bearing variant of the BE-0432 service_accounts shape). Two schema-level facts make this pattern structurally distinct from BE-0432: (i) api_keys.prefix is GLOBALLY UNIQUE (NOT UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix) — the prefix authentication lookup runs BEFORE the caller's tenant is known) so the BE-0432 "two tenants share a slug" worst-case fixture has no row-level analogue here; the cross-tenant prefix-conflict path is still load-bearing (orgA Insert with orgB's already-persisted prefix AND orgA RotateCredential to orgB's prefix) and BOTH paths must surface CodeConflict, leave orgB's bystander row byte-identical, and leave NO row anywhere carrying the rolled-back secret_hash needle (raw-SQL probe). (ii) api_keys carries a secret-bearing column — secret_hash — so the cross-tenant byte-identical-bystander assertion includes a `assertRawSecretHashUnchanged` raw-SQL probe AGAINST the typed-read SecretHash field (the typed read path is already covered by the struct snapshot, but a regression that scanned a non-secret column into the struct or skipped the field on Get would silently pass the struct check while the underlying row drifted — the raw probe is the load-bearing safety net for THAT class of regression specifically), and the cross-tenant indistinguishability test additionally asserts the secret_hash needle does NOT appear in any of the typed-error surfaces yeCross.Error() / yeCross.Message / yeCross.Hint across ALL five mutation methods (the secret-bearing redaction probe extension to the BE-0432 Code+Hint indistinguishability rule). Skeleton: one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) plus a 5-row indistinguishability-and-redaction table test that iterates (Get, UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, TouchLastUsed). Three load-bearing rules: (1) Every byte-identical-bystander test asserts BOTH the trigger-managed updated_at (the api_keys_set_updated_at BEFORE-UPDATE trigger refreshes it on every matched UPDATE, including a WHERE-on-id-only UPDATE that touched the wrong tenant) AND the lifecycle column the OWNING method writes (revoked_at for Revoke, last_used_at for TouchLastUsed, prefix+secret_hash for RotateCredential, name+scopes for UpdateMutable) so a WHERE-on-id-only regression surfaces independently on EITHER anchor. (2) The Insert cross-tenant prefix-conflict test must add a SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys global-table-size probe AFTER the conflicting Insert — a rolled-back Insert that landed its row in some OTHER tenant's slot would show up there even if the per-id probe missed it; the per-id probe alone is insufficient because the failing row's id is not known to the probe. (3) FindByPrefix is INTENTIONALLY NOT a cross-tenant probe surface — the signature carries no caller-tenant parameter, the prefix column is globally unique, and the resolved row carries its own organization_id; document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0426's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" note). Helper shape worth pinning forward: a `getXOrFail(ctx, t, s, repo, orgID, id, label) X` and a `writeX(ctx, s, fn) (X, error)` pair keep byte-identical snapshot tests free of Read/Write closure boilerplate; combined with a single `assertBystanderByteIdentical(t, label, baseline, after)` helper they reduce a 12-column equality check to two lines per (mutation × leg). Forward-applicable to BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant isolation — a child-table variant with NO secret-bearing column and only one mutation surface, so the secret-bearing redaction probe and most of the conflict-rollback shape collapse; the LIST-by-parent isolation rule remains) and to BE-0450 (variables tenant isolation, where the secret-bearing column is value WHEN is_secret=true, so the rolled-back-secret probe rule applies CONDITIONALLY on is_secret — the same shape extended with a per-row classification check). Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestAPIKeyRepository.*OrgADoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryGetReturnsCorrectRowAcrossTenants|TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryRotateCredentialCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByOrganizationCountsAreIsolated|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByServiceAccountIsTenantScoped|TestAPIKeyRepositoryCrossTenantErrorIsIndistinguishableFromUnknownId' ./internal/controlplane/store/ to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset. Same pre-existing -race-with-DB unrelated regressions noted under BE-0426/BE-0432 still apply — confirmed by stashing the new file and re-running, the failing tests are environment-service / api-key-service Create paths, organization_variable_patch audit metadata, and TestSQLArgsAreConstantExpressions; none are touched by this pattern.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1027:- PUT-contract four-test shape for /v1/<scope>/variables endpoints (BE-0146 pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/project_variables_put_contract_test.go): four tests — (1) server writes response data only through http.ResponseWriter (captureProcessOutput, NOT t.Parallel), (2) bearer-credential redaction in log buffer AND response body on BOTH success and deny legs (disabled-principal deny + err-on-call replacer to prove deny path is not the leak source), (3) submitted is_secret=true value redaction in log buffer AND response body on BOTH legs (the variables-WRITE-specific extra surface vs the read-only sibling), (4) dependency-cause leak pin including bare datastore address (apierr.StoreUnavailable wraps a "connection refused dialing 10.0.0.5:5432" cause; the typed 503 envelope must carry a stable generic message). The fourth test is the differentiator from the BE-0140 / BE-0143 read-only contract shape, which only needs three tests. The deny-leg redaction pin in test #3 is load-bearing: the deny path is precisely where a future regression that dumps the decoded body is most dangerous (the secret never reached the store but still reaches the operator log). Helper pattern: one replaceXxxHandlerForWithLogger that mirrors the existing replaceXxxHandlerFor with the final two NewHandler positional args swapped from (nil, nil) to (logger, nil). Constant pattern: three module-scope constants per file (bearer credential with endpoint-specific hex suffix, submitted secret marker with endpoint-specific hex suffix, canonical replace body that carries both a non-secret and a secret variable). Forward-applicable to every BE-0149..BE-0200 PUT-contract story whose request body can carry is_secret=true items.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1104:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfiguration that silently reduces the rotation pool is caught fast. (4) cmd/yalla-api wires the provider in ONE buildSecretsProvider helper near runStartupChecks, NOT one per scope. Strict profiles (staging/production) refuse to start without YALLA_SECRET_KEYS (config.requireStrictFields enforces) via an explicit sentinel error; permissive profiles (local/test) fall back to Plaintext with a Warn log line. (5) The interface is INTENTIONALLY same-width every scope needs (org/project/env/service variables), so a single provider configured at startup covers every variables table — sibling at-rest stories become mechanical clones of schema + service + test changes. (6) For internal-only read paths that legitimately need the plaintext (the future Dokploy renderer worker, BE-0343 variable-inheritance resolver), Open the sealed tuple through the same Provider passed into the service — NEVER a wire-facing endpoint. Six load-bearing rules for the storage shape per table: (1) Add three nullable columns (secret_provider text NULL, secret_key_id text NULL, secret_ciphertext bytea NULL) ALONGSIDE the existing plain `value` column, never instead of it; force value='' for secret rows and cipher columns NULL for non-secret rows via ONE CHECK constraint named <table>_secret_columns_consistent that pins the invariant in both directions. The CHECK is the defence-in-depth backstop the service relies on — a bug that tries to write a plaintext secret rolls back as deterministic apierr.Conflict rather than silent corruption. (2) Mirror DO $$ guards in BOTH the up and the down migration that REFUSE to run while is_secret=true rows exist with the wrong shape — a re-application against a database that accumulated plaintext secrets fails loudly rather than silently leaving them behind, and a rollback against a database with sealed rows fails loudly rather than silently losing every secret value. (3) Add a partial index on (secret_provider, secret_key_id) WHERE is_secret=true so the future key-rotation worker (BE-0351) can locate rows referencing a retired key without a full-table scan. (4) The repository's Upsert + UpdateMutable signatures take a closed-set Input tuple (<Table>VariableUpsert with Value/IsSecret/SecretProvider/SecretKeyID/SecretCiphertext) — keeps the signature narrow as new secret fields land and centralises the "nullable columns when is_secret=false" projection in one helper (nullableSecretColumns). (5) The OrganizationVariable struct (and siblings) gain the three encryption-at-rest fields as INTERNAL-only — never on the wire — and the LogValue redaction MUST be updated in the SAME edit to redact BOTH the plaintext AND the new ciphertext to output.Sentinel; an unstale slog record that captures the struct cannot leak either. (6) scanRow projection routes through sql.NullString for the nullable text columns (pgx-scan into *string rejects NULL). Five load-bearing rules for the service-layer seam: (1) Seal at the SERVICE layer, never the repository — Seal is a side-effecting call that depends on a runtime Provider not a *Tx, calling Seal BEFORE opening the transaction shortens the SQL critical section, and the repository stays pure SQL. The cleanest helper is a one-line projector (sealVariableValue) that takes the customer's input shape (<Table>VariableReplace) and returns the repository's tuple shape (<Table>VariableUpsert) — non-secret items pass through untouched, secret items go through Provider.Seal. (2) The Service's constructor (NewOrganizationVariableService) takes Provider as a REQUIRED dependency rejected at construction if nil — a production process cannot accidentally start with the at-rest seam disabled. (3) Patch path for sealed rows has THREE post-state cases the service must enumerate explicitly: (a) caller supplied a new value → use it verbatim; (b) caller supplied only is_secret AND the current row is_secret → Open the current ciphertext via Provider.Open to recover the plaintext, then re-Seal under the active key (the only correct way to re-key a secret without surfacing the plaintext to the caller); (c) otherwise → use the current row's `value` column. Skipping case (b) would either lose the secret bytes on a re-seal or write a "previously-sealed plaintext" string into the value column. (4) The demotion path (is_secret=true→false) reuses case (b) to recover the plaintext, then writes it back to the value column with NULL encryption columns — the ONLY customer path that surfaces a previously-sealed plaintext, and only as a deliberate operator action. (5) Seal failures surface as apierr.Internal with a value-free wrapped cause — a sealing error is a server-side problem, never customer input, and the wrap never echoes the plaintext. Six load-bearing rules for the integration-test surface: (1) The integration test file lives next to the existing repository tests as <table>_secrets_at_rest_test.go, uses testutil.RequireMigratedDB (skips cleanly when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres), and wires a REAL AES-256-GCM provider (mustAESGCM helper that mints a fresh 32-byte key per test so parallel tests cannot bleed key ids across each other). (2) Five pinned tests per scope: SealsSecretValuesAtRest (the load-bearing contract — secret value reaches Postgres ONLY as AES-GCM ciphertext under the active key id, value column is "", on-disk bytea does not contain the plaintext substring, ciphertext round-trips through Provider.Open, audit row never carries any of {plaintext, key name, hex ciphertext, key id, provider id}); PatchRotatesCiphertextOnEveryWrite (a patch that flips is_secret only on a sealed row Opens-then-Seals under a fresh nonce so on-disk bytes change between writes); PatchDemotesSecretToPlaintext (is_secret=true→false with no new value recovers the plaintext via Open and writes to the value column with NULL encryption columns); SchemaRejectsPlaintextSecretWrite (three direct INSERT attempts that would land a plaintext-secret row each surface