./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000010-20260518-165333.log:960:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an EXTERNAL code path (quota.Checker, not the repository), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO — ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) isolates rows by resource within a tenant); (c) the row-already-exists-with-non-zero-value leg has no policies analog — pre-seed via raw SQL at a sentinel value (e.g. used_value=17, id=`qu_seed_<resource>`) and prove BOTH the seeded id AND the seeded used_value survive a LockUsage call (this is the ONLY way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted prefix id does not replace the row's id — a regression to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would be a catastrophic data-loss path that typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to the typed Resource (e.g. `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")`) to bypass Go's compile-time check and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer — surfacing as yerr.CodeConflict through mapWriteError. The 8-new-test shape (row-shape, second-call-byte-identi