ince the trigger is BEFORE-UPDATE only, a rolled-back INSERT that resolved its UNIQUE check against the cross-tenant row would have no trigger anchor — Count is the second observable surface a row landing in the wrong tenant could leak through, mirroring the BE-0431 invariants-file Count-after-rollback rule); (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND Disable (the only two NotFound-emitting paths the repository exposes) — divergence in Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the mutation path is a separate code path from the read; afterward, re-read the foreign tenant's row and assert disabled_at == nil so the cross-tenant Disable probe did not silently flip the lifecycle. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run (mirrors BE-0430): (i) disjoint-fixture count isolation (orgA=1 SA, orgB=3 SAs, gotA must contain only saA's id, gotB must contain exactly {saB1, saB2, saB3} — proves no JOIN scrambled which rows land where); (ii) shared-slug-row isolation (saA and saB both with slug "shared-ci-slug", List(orgA) returns exactly one row with id=saA, List(orgB) returns exactly one row with id=saB — proves a SELECT disambiguating by slug instead of by organization would have either swapped or merged the two rows). One additional CountByOrganization-specific test seeds three tenants (orgA=2, orgB=3, orgC=0 empty bystander) and asserts the three distinct counts; this is the quota-layer leak surface (Count is the read the quota engine consults to enforce per-organization limits, so a leak inflates one tenant's effective quota with another's usage). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (service_accounts has no deletion_scheduled_at — only disabled_at, which the byte-identical Disable snapshot treats as the analogue) AND the deliberate ABSENCE of generic-Update / DELETE / cascade tests (Disable is the only mutation; the api_keys.service_account_id cross-tenant FK leak proof lives in servi