./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000077-20260519-042229.log:1016:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + READER ADAPTER + READER ADAPTER TEST ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND-ONLY WITH ONLY ONE WRITE SURFACE (Append) AND ONE READ SURFACE (ListByOrganization, no GetByID) AND A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE INSERT COLUMN LIST DELIBERATELY OMITS occurred_at + created_at (so DEFAULT now() applies and caller-supplied timestamps are SILENTLY DISCARDED)** (BE-0467 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_invariants_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0461 (provisioning_jobs) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the typed repository is the NARROWEST in the store package — Append + ListByOrganization only, NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown). The acceptance-criteria phrase "create typed repository methods for creating, reading, updating, and deleting ... where the lifecycle requires it" narrows to "Append + List only; the audit log lifecycle structurally rejects per-row Update / Delete" and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap; (b) the audit_events INSERT column list (auditEventColumns minus occurred_at/created_at) deliberately omits both timestamp columns, so DEFAULT now() applies regardless of caller. A test `TestAuditRepositoryAppendIgnoresCallerSuppliedTimestamps` is the regression backstop — a future refactor that added occurred_at to the INSERT (so a caller could back-date an audit record) would silently break the "audit timestamps are server-side" guarantee unless this test was also updated. The probe seeds OccurredAt=year-2000 and asserts the persisted row's OccurredAt is within [now-1s, now+1s], NOT the back-dated value; (c) CHECK constraints that the application does NOT pre-validate (blank action / blank resource_kind / blank reason / actor_kind outside the closed-set ('', 'usr', 'sa', 'system')) MUST be exercised: Append does NOT pre-check these fields (the audit service is the chokepoint for the principal-mapping inputs), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces as typed `apierr.Conflict` via mapWriteError. Each test asserts BOTH the error code AND `countAuditRowsForOrg(org.ID) == 0` to pin "the rejection left no row behind"; (d) the repository-level limit cap (`auditEventListMaxLimit = 200`) is a public invariant of the ListByOrganization contract and MUST be exercised in three branches — non-positive (limit <= 0 -> 200), above-cap (limit > 200 -> 200), in-range (limit in (0, 200] honoured verbatim). A regression that returned the empty slice when limit <= 0 (or every row when limit was unbounded) would surface here. The seeding helper `seedAuditRowsRawSQL` bypasses the repository and uses direct INSERT with caller-controlled occurred_at so 201 rows can be planted in O(n) without sleeping; (e) the secondary-sort ORDER BY id DESC tiebreaker on tied occurred_at is the load-bearing list-ordering invariant: a UI rendering a pair of audit events written in the same Postgres microsecond would flicker without it. The probe seeds TWO rows with the SAME occurred_at (via raw INSERT — Append's DEFAULT now() would advance between calls) and asserts listed[0].ID > listed[1].ID lexicographically. The 16-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + aud_ prefix + server-side timestamps within [before, after]), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-ignores-caller-supplied-timestamps (the load-bearing server-side-timestamp probe), four CHECK-violation Conflicts (blank-action, blank-resource_kind, blank-reason, unknown-actor_kind), duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-transaction-rollback (sentinel error + countAuditRowsForOrg == 0), Append-cascades-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/organization_id/action/reason/occurred_at/created_at), Append-peer-row-byte-identical (the BE-0468 byte-identity bystander predecessor — peer Append must NOT touch a prior row), List-orders-id-DESC-on-timestamp-tie, three List-limit-clamping branches (non-positive, above-cap, in-range), List-unknown-org-returns-empty. New helpers (per-file Audit-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg (the per-org counter — organization is the only parent FK for audit_events, so this is also the natural per-parent counter), mintAuditID, auditTxRollbackSentinel, seedAuditRowsRawSQL (the raw-INSERT batch seeder for cap-boundary tests). Reused verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit, assertAuditEventByteIdentical; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table is append-only with a single (Append, List) surface and a repository-level limit cap (a future security_events / system_audit table would inherit the same template, including the server-side-timestamp invariant if the INSERT column list omits the timestamp legs).
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000077-20260519-042229.log:1017:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE HAS A CLAIM PROTOCOL (Claim + Complete + Release + Find — three writes, one read) DRIVEN BY AN UPSERT WITH AN ON-CONFLICT TARGET ON A NON-PK UNIQUE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES A MULTI-COLUMN COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK** (BE-0469 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0467 (audit_events) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the Claim INSERT uses `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) DO UPDATE` rather than the canonical `ON CONFLICT (id)` -- the PK is the secondary disambiguator, not the conflict target. So a duplicate id on a DIFFERENT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple is NOT caught by ON CONFLICT; the PK violation flows through the database and surfaces through mapWriteError as apierr.Conflict. The TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimDuplicateIDIsConflict probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on different (principal, key) tuples; a regression that switched the ON CONFLICT target to (id) would swallow the collision and silently rewrite a peer row. (b) The `idempotency_keys_completion_consistent` CHECK requires (response_status, response_body, completed_at) to be either ALL NULL (pending) or ALL NOT NULL (completed). The Complete method coerces a nil `body []byte` argument to `[]byte{}` (zero-length but non-nil) BEFORE the UPDATE so the CHECK's `response_body IS NOT NULL` clause holds for empty envelopes. TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteCoercesNilBodyToEmpty pins both the application-layer coercion AND the database-side non-NULL empty-bytea storage. The pending-side of the CHECK is pinned by TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimMintsRowShape (a fresh Claim must leave all three completion columns NULL); the completed-side by TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteRowShape (after Complete, all three must be non-NULL AND a raw-SQL re-load must byte-equal the returned struct, AND created_at + expires_at must NOT have drifted across the Complete UPDATE -- only the four completion columns transition). (c) The Complete method's `now time.Time` argument is the only application-controlled timestamp on the table: created_at + expires_at are caller-supplied via Claim and completed_at can be caller-supplied via Complete's now arg (zero defaults to time.Now().UTC()). TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteHonoursExplicitNow uses a specific 2024 timestamp far from time.Now() so a regression that ignored the arg and wrote time.Now() would fail with an obvious delta. This is a structurally distinct invariant from BE-0467 (audit_events) where ALL timestamps are server-side via DEFAULT now() and a caller-supplied OccurredAt must be discarded. (d) The id minter newIdempotencyID emits `idk_<32hex>` (16 bytes of crypto/rand entropy hex-encoded — 4 + 32 = 36 chars total). The row-shape probe MUST pin BOTH the prefix AND the total length so a regression that shortened the entropy or reformatted the prefix surfaces here. Test-local mintIdempotencyID(t, suffix) follows the established per-file suffixed-helper pattern (`idk_`+t.Name()+`_`+suffix) so parallel tests that need a deterministic caller-supplied id (to force a PK collision) cannot collide. (e) The Release method has NO pre-validation coverage in the primary idempotency_test.go's TestIdempotencyClaimValidation (which only covers Claim nil-tx + Claim missing-fields + Complete nil-tx + Complete out-of-range-status). The invariants file fills the Release gap with TestIdempotencyRepositoryReleaseRejectsNilTx so all three mutating methods (Claim / Complete / Release) have a pinned nil-tx guard. The 15-test shape this file lands: Claim-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + idk_ prefix +