./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000077-20260519-042229.log:1050:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, THREE distinct UPDATE paths (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential), an additional UPDATE-but-not-mutation-state path (TouchLastUsed), and a secret-bearing row body (secret_hash + globally-UNIQUE prefix)** (BE-0433 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go, BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go, and BE-0431 serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but five substitutions are mandatory because api_keys has no version column, three UPDATE paths instead of one, a separate touch-style UPDATE for the auth path, and a secret-bearing row body: (a) the trigger-dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE UPDATE trigger) must run ONCE PER UPDATE PATH — UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, and TouchLastUsed each get their own dedicated dual-anchor test, so a refactor that dropped the trigger from one path but not the others trips a path-specific gate (the BE-0431 single-Disable pattern is too weak here); (b) the UpdateMutable dual-anchor test additionally pins the **mutable surface** to exactly {name, scopes} by snapshotting every non-mutable column (prefix, secret_hash, created_by, service_account_id, expires_at, revoked_at, last_used_at) as byte-identical to the baseline — UpdateMutable is the first repository method in the store package whose SET-clause is a true subset of the table's mutable columns, so the non-mutation assertion is the only structural defense against a future refactor that quietly widened the SET-clause; (c) RotateCredential gets a dedicated test that pins the **credential swap** invariant: prefix and secret_hash flip to the new values, every identity/ownership/scopes/lifecycle column stays byte-identical, the OLD pr