 - Security-verification cross-package secret-confinement pattern, Dokploy-token instance (BE-0361 pattern, internal/release/dokploy_token_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/config/dokploy_token_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Dokploy Token Isolation" section + internal/controlplane/dokploy/AGENTS.md "Token isolation (BE-0361)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST cross-package instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346) and "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF) to "this scarce in-process secret MUST flow from source-of-truth package A through redaction package B into wire-emission package C with NO leak surface in customer-facing package D". Six load-bearing rules: (1) TRAILING-SELECTOR-NAME matching — when the field name is unambiguously the secret in the codebase ("DokployToken" is unique to the Config secret field, no other struct has a field of the same name), match by `SelectorExpr.Sel.Name == "DokployToken"` regardless of the receiver. This catches `config.DokployToken`, `cfg.DokployToken`, `c.DokployToken`, and any future-receiver shape alike, in ONE matcher rule, where the package-qualified-only variant would miss the `c.DokployToken` form a method using a `*config.Config` receiver would naturally write. The receiver-name freedom is the load-bearing rule: any closed list of qualifiers (`config.`, `cfg.`, …) is brittle to local-naming choices in a future refactor. (2) RETURN-FALSE-FROM-INSPECT to suppress double-counting when a matcher fires on a parent node whose children would ALSO match — `config.EnvDokployToken` is both a SelectorExpr (X=`config`, Sel=`EnvDokployToken`) and contains an inner `EnvDokployToken` Ident that the bare-Ident matcher would also flag. Returning `false` from the `ast.Inspect` callback after emitting the SelectorExpr diagnostic stops descent and keeps the count at 1. The self-