faces — `(*Config).LogValue()` MUST call `c.Redacted()` first AND MUST NOT touch `c.DokployToken` directly. The matcher walks the method body, tracking `seenRedactedCall` (set when a `c.Redacted()` CallExpr is found) and emitting a diagnostic for EVERY `c.DokployToken` SelectorExpr seen. The two diagnostics are independent — a method that called `c.Redacted()` but ALSO read `c.DokployToken` directly would fire one; a method that omitted the call but had no direct read would fire the other; a method that did both would fire both. Forward-applicable to any "MUST consult helper H before reading secret S" pattern (variables LogValue, future credentials redactor, etc.). (6) MARKER-PATTERN runtime gate at the projection seams — three table-driven tests in `dokploy_token_isolation_test.go` install a fixed `BE0361DOKPLOYTOKENMARKERXYZ` prefix on the Config.DokployToken field and assert the marker never appears in (a) the `json.Marshal(c.Redacted())` byte stream, (b) the `slog.JSONHandler` output under `slog.Any("config", c)`, and (c) the `c.String()` debug projection — for EACH of three fixtures: short-token (canonical), long-token (longer than the redactor's sentinel, longer than any reasonable line-buffered slog chunk so a partial-flush leak would still surface), and collision-prefix (token prefix collides with the projection's field name `dokploy_token`, so a regression that truncated at the field-name boundary would still trip the marker check). The marker is the leak detector — asserting on token bytes alone would false-positive whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the stable projection text. (7) GOLANGCI-LINT S1025 gotcha — staticcheck (under golangci-lint v2) flags `fmt.Sprintf("%s", x)` as redundant when `x` has a `String()` method, suggesting `x.String()` instead. For BE-0361 the intent of the runtime test was to PROVE that `fmt.Sprintf("%s", cfg)` doesn't leak, but since `fmt.Sprintf` with `%s` against a Stringer DELEGATES to `.String()`, calling `.S